\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Problem 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x_{t};t\geq0\}$
\end_inset

 is a markov chain with transition matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{n\times n}$
\end_inset

.
 A 
\series bold
stationary (or invariant)
\series default
 distribution for 
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

 is a probability vector such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}=\pi^{\prime}P$
\end_inset

.
 Find out the invariant distribution for
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0.7 & 0.3\\
0.4 & 0.6
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0.9 & 0.1
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0.7 & 0.3\\
0.4 & 0.6
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}=\pi^{\prime}P\iff\pi^{\prime}(I_{2}-P)=0\iff\left(I_{2}-P^{\prime}\right)\pi=0$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\pi_{1}\\
\pi_{2}
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(I_{2}-P^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0.3 & -0.4\\
-0.3 & 0.4
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\left(I_{2}-P^{\prime}\right)|\ 0\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0.3 & -0.4\\
-0.3 & 0.4
\end{array}\left|\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0
\end{array}\right.\right]\sim\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & -\frac{4}{3}\\
0 & 0
\end{array}\left|\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0
\end{array}\right.\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\pi_{1}\\
\pi_{2}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\frac{4}{3}\pi_{2}\\
\pi_{2}
\end{array}\right]=\pi_{2}\left[\begin{array}{c}
\frac{4}{3}\\
1
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}+\pi_{2}=1$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{4}{3}\pi_{2}+\pi_{2}=1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies\pi_{2}=\frac{3}{7}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\frac{4}{7}\\
\frac{3}{7}
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(b) 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 0\\
0.9 & 0.1
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{\prime}=\pi^{\prime}P\iff\pi^{\prime}(I_{2}-P)=0\iff\left(I_{2}-P^{\prime}\right)\pi=0$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\pi_{1}\\
\pi_{2}
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(I_{2}-P^{\prime})=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & -0.9\\
0 & 0.9
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\left[\left(I_{2}-P^{\prime}\right)|\ 0\right]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & -0.9\\
0 & 0.9
\end{array}\left|\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0
\end{array}\right.\right]\sim\left[\begin{array}{cc}
0 & 0\\
0 & 1
\end{array}\left|\begin{array}{c}
0\\
0
\end{array}\right.\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\pi_{1}\\
\pi_{2}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\pi_{1}\\
0
\end{array}\right]=\pi_{1}\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{1}+\pi_{2}=1$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Problem 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A shopper wants to purchase one unit of an indivisible consumption good
 at the lowest possible cost.
 There are multiple stores that she can visit.
 First, there is a small neighborhood convenience store.
 In addition, there is a mall with an infinite number of stores in it.
 Each of these stores offers the good at a random price.
 For each store, the price is drawn independently from the same uniform
 distribution over the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,B]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The agent can follow one of two strategies.
 Either she can go immediately to the mall start searching there (strategy
 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

), or she can first check the convenience store and continue to the mall
 only if she is not satisfied with the local price (strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cost of traveling to the convenience store and examining the price there
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Once at the convenience store, the agent holding the item with the price
 tag 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 has two choices.
 She can either purchase the item and stop shopping, or she can leave the
 item and continue to the mall.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The cost of traveling to the mall, either from home or from the convenience
 store is 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{2}>c_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 (This includes the cost of checking the price in the first store in the
 mall.) Again, the shopper can either accept this first price and stop shopping
 or she can reject it and go to the next store in the mall.
 The cost of going to the next store and echking the price there is 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon<c_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 In the mall, she can continue shopping as long as she wishes.
 Visiting each new store costs an additional amount 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon<\frac{B}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The agent aims to 
\series bold
minimize
\series default
 the total cost of shopping, which is the sum of travel costs an thep rice
 that is ultimately paid.
 You can assume that there is no recall of past prices.
 Note also that there is no discounting.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(a) Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}(p)$
\end_inset

 denotes the value of expected future costs for the shopper if she is in
 one of the stores in the mall holding an item priced 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 .
 Describe the Bellman equation.
 Show that her optimal strategy is to follow a reservation price strategy,
 i.e., buy the item if 
\begin_inset Formula $p\leq\bar{p}_{2}$
\end_inset

, otherwise continue shopping.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The shopper wishes to devise a strategy to minimize her total cost of shopping
 in the mall, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $TC=p+N\epsilon$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $N=\left[\mbox{number of stores visited in the mall}-1\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}(p)$
\end_inset

 be the expected value of the cost to the shopper, who is at a store in
 the mall and has price 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 in hand.
 She is deciding whether to accept or to reject it, and she behaves optimally.
 We assume no recall.
 The value function 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}(p)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Bellman equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}(p)=\min\left\{ p,\epsilon+\int_{0}^{B}p^{\prime}dF(p^{\prime})\right\} $
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(2.1)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the minimization is over the two actions: 
\begin_inset Formula $(1)$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
accept
\emph default
 the price offer 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $(2)$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
reject
\emph default
 the price, spend 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 to go to the next store, and draw a new offer 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 from distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
 Figure 
\begin_inset Formula $(2.1)$
\end_inset

 below graphs the functional equation 
\begin_inset Formula $(2.1)$
\end_inset

 and reveals that its solution will be of the form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $v_{2}(p)=\begin{cases}
p & \mbox{if}\ p\leq\bar{p}_{2}\\
\bar{p}_{2}=\epsilon+\int_{0}^{B}p^{\prime}dF(p^{\prime}) & \mbox{if}\ p\geq\bar{p}_{2}
\end{cases}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(2.2)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

